{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/779769-roleplaying-xenophobia","id":779769,"title":"Roleplaying Xenophobia","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-12-04T07:01:05-08:00","id":779769,"title":"Roleplaying Xenophobia","content":"In this era of social justice and inclusiveness, it's becoming easier and easier to fall into the omni-acceptable omni-sexual tropes of <s>Mass Effect, D&amp;D and Exalted</s> game worlds.<br /> <br /> I wanted to talk about being inclusive towards <i>players </i>of all kinds, and depicting xenophobia of various degrees towards <i>characters</i>, and how to showcase these in a respectful way towards players. There's already more than enough horror stories about gorilla-gamers rapping female characters every game session hanging around, and that's not what I want to discuss.<br /> <br /> For example: my gaming groups consists mainly of LGBT members, and their characters, by default, usually mirror them in many ways, which is fine. But in my most recent game of Exalted, I included a city in which same-sex marriage is denied to the nobility, because that would mean losing a branch of the family tree. Marriage between nobility and Dragon-Blooded is also forbidden, since the blood of the dragons is stronger and would crush out the blood of nobility.<br /> <br /> In a Promethean game I was running, one of my player decided to struggle with his character's sexual identity, and thus he was being bullied for being a [REDACTED HOMOPHOBIC SLUR] and an aberration towards god by a few select characters.<br /> <br /> As a storyteller, I always try to make sure my players understand my stand in these situations. In my Exalted game, I explained the Nobility's thinking, and in the Promethean game, I described the bullies as bigots, and I was monitoring my player's reactions as much as I could. It created nice moments in which my players felt like they were dealing with their past in a controlled environment, being empowered in many ways. Sure, one of them shed a single tear, but when I asked him if he'd prefer we fade to black and call it a day, he continued and dealt with the bigots himself.<br /> <br /> So, what are your stories of xenophobia inside a game rather than outside of it?<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> EDIT: You know what? Let's not start another thread about inclusiveness... it's causing enough havoc as is...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Glorious Useless-Post Prana<br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Cost: 2</span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">m; </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Mins: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Linguistics 1, Essence 1 </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Type: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Simple</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Keywords: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Mute</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Duration: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Instant</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Prerequisite Charms: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Ten-Characters Methodology</span>","edited":{"userid":2589,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2589-magnum-opus","user":"Magnum Opus","date":". 12-04-2015, 01:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2589-magnum-opus","name":"Magnum Opus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2589_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"821"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-04T13:02:30-08:00","id":780075,"title":"","content":"I think Indie Games are the best place to explore this topic, as the players usually know what they are getting into with an indie game rather than including it in a Mainstream game. I really like exploring different moralities than I hold to try to understand WHY people think like that. I think hate and fear of the unfamiliar is a topic that should be explored, so we can understand why people think that way. <br /> <br /> I ran a short <a href=\"http://www.lumpley.com/dogs.html\" target=\"_blank\">Dogs in the Vineyard</a> campaign about a town called Angel's Rest where a girl died of a blotched abortion. In that game, the PCs (not the players) fell on the side of religious fanatics and executed the Abortionist in the middle of town for her crimes. They scolded the girl's father (who was one of the town elders) for trying to hide the fact that his daughter was engaging in premartial sex instead of marrying her off as was proper. It was a very dark roleplaying experience and one that the players bought into when they agreed to play the game. We had a talk afterwards about the different social issues involved in the game (The father just got a stern talking to when he was ignoring his daughter's sexuality for political gain, the abortionist that probably saved more lives than harmed was killed, etc)<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://gregorhutton.com/boxninja/threesixteen/\" target=\"_blank\">Three Sixteen</a> is the ultimate &quot;Killed the Aliens&quot; game. You can do a lot within the game's simple framework to discuss how war warps you and how you can learn to dehumanize the enemy. I ran my one shot Three Sixteen game as an Avatar Sequel. They players were humans that came back to &quot;kill all the blue monkeys.&quot; That game didn't have quite as much Social Justice in it, but we could have added it in. <br /> <br /> I feel like a bad person for running these games now. I swear I'm not a horrible person! I just play one in RPG games!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I write things.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3950-wonderandawe","name":"wonderandawe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3950_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5410"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-04T19:42:48-08:00","id":780319,"title":"","content":"Once, for a lark, I had my team of superhero PCs fight a collection of street gangs - the BZs, the Arkhams, and Dev's Keepers. All of them were multiethnic gangs to avoid the stereotype of a bunch of black or latino guys in saggy pants being the &quot;bad guys.&quot;<br /> <br /> Now, as a spur-of-the-moment thing, I decided to make the BZ's gimmick that they were super-progressive. In addition to being multi-ethnic, two of their male members were married to each other, and they showed universal acceptance of any person who accepted their creed of &quot;get money from other people by selling drugs and threatening violence.&quot; Their territory was covered with diversity murals and upbeat slogans even as they tried to co-opt high school kids into crime. Everyone thought it was hilarious.<br /> <br /> I decided to follow up on that success by making the the Arkham's gimmick that they were really apathetic race/religion/ethnicity - their turf was tagged with black and grey and Nietsche quotes. This, too, was funny.<br /> <br /> But when they got Dev's Keepers, I had their gimmick be that they were indisciminately hostile to everyone, including themselves. Their turf was tagged with an alphabetized list of every racial, religious, or ethnic slur I could find. One player was extremely upset by this.<br /> <br /> I talked to them later and found that their problem was with the language itself - the idea of a universal hategroup was ok, and I could even use made-up verbal abuses if I wanted. But apparently using the known vocabulary of hate to make the joke ruined it for them. I'm not sure what to make of that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-04T20:48:30-08:00","id":780360,"title":"","content":"I've found the Iron Kingdoms setting &quot;good&quot; for this, as there's so much -ism between the various countries, races, species and nationalities... although admittedly I do tend to strive to do so with humour. For example one character was the last scion of an offshoot branch of what amounted to a noble dwarven family. With the catch that his family died and he largely raised himself in the capital city of one of the primarily human empires, resulting in him developing a wicked streak of bigoted hate for dwarves.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-04T22:19:47-08:00","id":780393,"title":"","content":"(Whoa, I'm actually participating in constructive conversation. Yay me?)<br /> <br /> <br /> I'll speak purely from experience here, because I'm probably the dumbest person on this forum. Anyway, my tabletop experience over the years, in terms of IC/OOC xenophobia, has varied greatly. I've always been an inclusive person when it comes to my gaming table. I've been the ST in about 95% of all the tabletop I've ever played, so that usually means &quot;whoever stays&quot; is my default audience and player pool. That makes for interesting situations. Some very uncomfortable, some comfortable as a couch that whispers positive affirmations while giving neck massages.<br /> <br /> I started running games in a junior high school where a good quarter of the students were functionally illiterate. I was the only goth kid for miles and nobody really talked to my bookworm-self much. That was until, spurred on by an old black silk tophat (iirc), I started cracking open my game books and enticing all comers with promises of bloodshed, monsters and awesomeness (aka the cWoD). I'd set up at a lunch table and people who were punching my head to assuage their boredom a week before were playing Brujah cracking skulls for the Anarch cause in the shadows beyond the streetlights alongside bored cheerleaders playing equally bored Toreador (with surprising gusto. Apparently even Mean Girls have their place at the table) and my meager group of friends.<br /> As you can imagine, there were some differences in what this Hodge podge of humans thought of as appropriate. I've had players so homophobic that they would later commit hate crimes. I really wish I was kidding. I've also had lgbt players of all flavors at those same tables playing everything from Mary Sue characters to their own polar opposites and every combination in between. Sometimes the only arguments that occurred were IC. Sometimes they were shitstorms that led to West Side Story-esque rumbles (Just picture the school from Dangerous Minds and add a Jr high prequel scene.).<br /> As an adult, the random groups I'd cobbled together over the years eventually crumbled. Some moved, some had kids and careers to tend to. The rest is public record, probably. (Did I mention criminality was common?)<br /> <br /> I started going online for games after a few years of moping around and missing my lovely tabletop. It's been a mixed bag as well. I've had players who refuse to curse in-character after defining their character as an amoral sociopath. I've had players who, regardless of their own race, fucking Love to roar out some hate speech in-character. One player, (whose black ass shall remain nameless, Troy. Oops, totally meant..Troy. it's Troy.) relishes a racist rant so much, when it's done in proper context, that I eventually set a game in the early seventies just so his character could be a terrible, terrible human being in a setting where overt racism was permissible while also being a great Hunter. (That game is ongoing. God help us all.)<br /> <br /> Am I racist for allowing racist characters or homophobia into my storylines? As a bisexual Mexican-American...yeah. That is, if you judge me only by the worlds I build and the worst among the characters that inhabit them. Otherwise, no. It doesn't. Playing with worlds, and people, that don't exist is a good tool to examine those viewpoints, though. It's a great way to examine unfamiliar viewpoints in general. Have some of my players been socially unacceptable idiots? Yeah. Those exist, so my voracious table hath sampled them. Have I had thoughtful, intelligent and creative people play characters that eerily resemble those idiots? Also, yeah. The experience desired varies from time to time and player to player. Sometimes the straight-edge Christian rock guy wants to play an agnostic bisexual Toreador girl with &quot;daddy issues&quot;.<br /> Sometimes a college professor wants to play a werewolf named Pietro who has more booty than brains. Half the female players I've ever had have played male characters almost exclusively, some of which were some grotesquely misogynistic rapemonkey-buttholes indeed.<br /> <br /> Really, if you can't explore the darker, sleazier side of the human mind, why play games that are &quot;realistic&quot; in any way? The worlds I play in, mostly courtesy of WW/OPP, weren't meant to be inoffensive. They're culturally and sexually inclusive, in many ways. I'll grant the OP that. They're also culturally biased IC, full of allegorical racism, cultural intolerance and classism. The WoD(s) imo showcase this best, but there's a bit of the worst parts of the human condition in most good, mature RPGs. It's there because it's here, with us in the real world. As anyone who likes fiction enough could tell you; a story with a foot in something REAL can make the difference between a good story and a bad one. The best lies are nearly always closest to the truth. That's fiction for ya. A bad story to me is an emotionally dishonest one. A story that trades heart for harmony.<br /> \u200b Who knows, maybe I've been doing things all wrong. In the end though, I think I'll take my xenophobia, racism, homophobia and other forms of offensive ignorance in fictional form over the real thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:times new roman\"><span style=\"color:#0000CD\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><b>\u201cNobody is purely good or purely evil. Mo</b></span><b>st of us are in-between. There are moths that explore the day and butterflies that play at night.\u201d </b><br /> - Suzy Kassem, Rise Up and Salute The Sun<br /> <b>(She/Her)</b></span></span>","edited":{"userid":380,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/380-azraelfirestorm","user":"AzraelFirestorm","date":".  I'm pretty sure most of this is gibberish, but I can't tell, because crazy 12-05-2015, 04:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/380-azraelfirestorm","name":"AzraelFirestorm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar380_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-05T08:57:36-08:00","id":780575,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AzraelFirestorm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/779769-roleplaying-xenophobia?p=780393#post780393\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Really, if you can't explore the darker, sleazier side of the human mind, why play games that are &quot;realistic&quot; in any way? The worlds I play in, mostly courtesy of WW/OPP, weren't meant to be inoffensive. They're culturally and sexually inclusive, in many ways. I'll grant the OP that. They're also culturally biased IC, full of allegorical racism, cultural intolerance and classism. The WoD(s) imo showcase this best, but there's a bit of the worst parts of the human condition in most good, mature RPGs. It's there because it's here, with us in the real world. As anyone who likes fiction enough could tell you; a story with a foot in something REAL can make the difference between a good story and a bad one. The best lies are nearly always closest to the truth. That's fiction for ya. A bad story to me is an emotionally dishonest one. A story that trades heart for harmony.<br /> Who knows, maybe I've been doing things all wrong. In the end though, I think I'll take my xenophobia, racism, homophobia and other forms of offensive ignorance in fictional form over the real thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>This. By far, my favorite line is called <b>the World of Darkness</b>. It's like our own, only filled with desperation and a vague, silent understanding that things from our collective nightmares are pulling strings in the dark. So, things are actually worse in there than they are in real life. How <i>much</i> <i>worse</i> is up to you, but things are not supposed to be better. I come from a mixed racial background, plus I have the sexuality and morality of a pagan, so yeah I don't mind running or playing scenes that challenge my own lifestyles.<br /> <br /> And remember kids, there's one issue so taboo no one does anything about it! It's called wealth inequality, and it's friends with economic exclusion and exploitation. All it takes for most of us to stop caring, is for the billionaires to make tax-exempt donations to an offshore charity or company! (a friendly reminder from your local Carthian coterie)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-05T11:17:06-08:00","id":780634,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AzraelFirestorm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/779769-roleplaying-xenophobia?p=780393#post780393\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Really, if you can't explore the darker, sleazier side of the human mind, why play games that are &quot;realistic&quot; in any way? The worlds I play in, mostly courtesy of WW/OPP, weren't meant to be inoffensive. They're culturally and sexually inclusive, in many ways. I'll grant the OP that. They're also culturally biased IC, full of allegorical racism, cultural intolerance and classism. The WoD(s) imo showcase this best, but there's a bit of the worst parts of the human condition in most good, mature RPGs. It's there because it's here, with us in the real world.</b> As anyone who likes fiction enough could tell you; a story with a foot in something REAL can make the difference between a good story and a bad one. The best lies are nearly always closest to the truth. That's fiction for ya. A bad story to me is an emotionally dishonest one. A story that trades heart for harmony.<br /> \u200b Who knows, maybe I've been doing things all wrong. In the end though, I think I'll take my xenophobia, racism, homophobia and other forms of offensive ignorance in fictional form over the real thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Very well said. And I agree completely.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">&quot;Steel isn't strong, boy. Flesh is stronger.&quot;</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/95-fat-larry","name":"Fat Larry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar95_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1337"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}